WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:09.580
SPEAKER: Data is often
scattered in many places.

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:11.560
You may store customer
tables in BigQuery,

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:14.320
while your sales tables
live in Cloud SQL.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:15.940
Nonetheless, it
is important to be

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:17.530
able to join these
tables together

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:19.390
when performing analysis.

00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:21.550
Cloud SQL Federation
from BigQuery

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:23.680
enables you to
analyze data residing

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:27.980
in Cloud SQL in real time,
without copying or moving data.

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:30.760
It supports both MySQL
and Postgres instances,

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:32.229
and Cloud SQL.

00:00:32.229 --> 00:00:34.090
After an initial
one-time setup, you

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:36.700
can write queries referencing
connected Cloud SQL

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:40.780
tables using a special SQL
function called External Query.

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:43.420
Let's walk through the steps
to complete the initial setup,

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:45.550
and then run an example query.

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:46.360
Step 1.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:48.760
Enable the BigQuery
connection service.

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:50.650
Start in the Cloud
console, and select

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:53.050
the project that includes
the Cloud SQL instance

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:54.507
that you want to query.

00:00:54.507 --> 00:00:56.590
If you would like to start
with the same Cloud SQL

00:00:56.590 --> 00:00:58.690
instance we are using
in this Quick Start,

00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:01.390
you may pause the video and
first follow the codelab linked

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:03.440
in the description below.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:06.160
In this project, you will need
to have both the BigQuery admin

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:09.880
role and the Cloud SQL editor
or Cloud SQL admin role.

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:13.570
Use the left-hand menu to
click on APIs and Services.

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:16.510
Click Enable APIs and Services.

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:19.480
And then search for
BigQuery connection API.

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:21.830
Click to enable.

00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:22.610
Step 2.

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:26.690
Configure public IP connectivity
for your Cloud SQL instance.

00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:30.650
Use the search bar to
navigate to Cloud SQL.

00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:35.110
Click the instance name
to open its details page.

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:38.130
Select the connections tab.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:41.280
Make sure the public
IP checkbox is marked.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:44.640
While public IP connectivity
is not as secure as private IP

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.250
only, your instance
will remain inaccessible

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:49.200
from the public internet,
because you have not

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:51.330
provided an authorized address.

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:53.460
However, this will make
it possible to connect

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:55.710
to your instance from BigQuery.

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:58.610
Click Save, if you made changes.

00:01:58.610 --> 00:01:59.570
Step 3.

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:03.110
Set up the Cloud SQL database
connection in BigQuery.

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:05.780
Use the search bar to
navigate to BigQuery.

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:07.070
Click Add Data.

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:09.840
And select External Data Source.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.080
Now you'll provide the
details needed to establish

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:13.640
the connection resource.

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:16.670
Choose your database
type-- in this case, MySQL.

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:18.410
Give the connection ID and name.

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:20.840
You will use this identifier
when you are referencing

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:22.910
the resource from a query.

00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:25.970
The location you choose must be
compatible with your Cloud SQL

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:29.060
region and your intended
use within BigQuery.

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:32.450
Since our Cloud SQL database
is located in US Central one,

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:35.600
we will need to use the
US multi region location.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:37.940
More information on
location compatibility

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:40.740
is linked in the
description below.

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:42.960
Enter the full name of
the Cloud SQL instance

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:46.350
in the format of project ID,
colon, location ID, colon,

00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:47.880
instance ID.

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:50.730
You can find the instance ID on
the details page of the Cloud

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:53.650
SQL instance you want to query.

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:56.740
For database name, enter
the name of the database.

00:02:56.740 --> 00:03:00.010
Then enter the username and
password for the database.

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:02.110
Note-- if these same
user credentials are

00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:06.200
valid for other databases in the
particular Cloud SQL instance,

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:07.960
the connection will
also allow queries

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:10.690
over those additional databases
through the same connection

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:12.010
resource.

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:13.960
Click Create Connection.

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:17.700
You'll see the connection
populate in the left-hand nav.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:18.720
Step 4.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:21.240
Grant permissions
to connection users.

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:24.030
Select the connection in the
left-hand nav and click Share

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:25.140
Connection.

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:27.810
Enter the user's address and
select BigQuery Connection

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:29.790
User, which gives
the user the ability

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:31.890
to query with the connection.

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:33.720
Alternatively, you
can give a user

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:35.610
the role BigQuery
connection admin,

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:38.670
which allows them to create,
manage, and share connections,

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:41.318
in addition to using
them in queries.

00:03:41.318 --> 00:03:43.110
Now that we've completed
the initial setup,

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:45.150
let's see how to write
queries over tables

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:46.980
in the connected database.

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:50.040
Our first query joins the
employees and salaries tables,

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:53.520
both in the Cloud SQL database,
to analyze the average salary

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:54.900
by year of hire.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:56.670
Querying over the
external connection

00:03:56.670 --> 00:03:59.220
requires the external
query function.

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:01.810
The syntax of this function
requires the connection ID,

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:03.870
which you can find by
selecting the connection

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:05.850
and looking in the details.

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:09.210
Next is a string of the query
in the external databases SQL

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:10.740
dialect.

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:13.170
Note that when querying
a Cloud SQL connection,

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:16.300
BigQuery can not predict the
amount of data to be processed.

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:19.620
You will only know after you run
your query, when you hit Run.

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:23.640
The query is executed in the
external database in Cloud SQL.

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:26.010
Next, let's see an example
of joining a BigQuery

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:28.710
table with a table in
your Cloud SQL database.

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:31.110
We have taken a snapshot
of the salaries table

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:32.670
and loaded it into
BigQuery's, so

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:35.700
that we can show joining data
across BigQuery and Cloud SQL

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:36.750
resources.

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:39.450
The example query
includes three parts.

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:42.270
External query is used to
select the employee number

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:45.810
and hire date fields from the
Cloud SQL employees table.

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:48.600
Then, the results are
joined by employee number

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:51.720
with the salaries table
which is located in BigQuery.

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:54.360
Finally, the query
calculates the average salary

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.790
for employees in each hire year.

00:04:56.790 --> 00:04:57.600
Click Run.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:00.760
And after the query completes,
you'll see the same results.

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:03.090
The cost of your queries
will follow standard BigQuery

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:05.700
pricing, either on demand,
based on the amount of data

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:07.860
processed, or as part
of a flat rate model

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:10.770
if that is applicable
to your organization.

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:13.590
You've just seen how to set
up a Cloud SQL connection

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:16.920
and query the connection using
the external query function.

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:18.900
For more information
on Federated queries

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:21.690
with Cloud SQL, please
navigate to the documentation

00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:22.770
linked below.

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:24.000
Happy analyzing.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:26.750
[MUSIC PLAYING]

